\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Autoregressive language model}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{language-model}
\tag{transformer}

\p{Let #{\mathcal V} be a finite vocabulary. An \strong{autoregressive
language model} with parameters #{\theta\in\Theta} assigns, to every finite
token prefix #{x_{\leq t}\in\mathcal V^t}, a probability distribution}

##{
  \pi_\theta(\mathord\cdot\mid x_{\leq t})\in\Delta(\mathcal V).
}

\p{The probability of a continuation #{x_{t+1:T}} is the causal product}

##{
  \pi_\theta(x_{t+1:T}\mid x_{\leq t})
  =\prod_{s=t+1}^{T}\pi_\theta(x_s\mid x_{<s}).
}

\p{An \strong{architecture} specifies how the prefix is encoded into hidden
states and logits. In a decoder-only Transformer, token and position
representations pass through a causal stack of attention and feed-forward
blocks, then through a normalization and language-model head before softmax.
The attention architecture follows \citef{vaswani2017attention}; normalization
choices are exemplified by \citef{ba2016layer}. Modern sparse or recurrent
variants may change the stack without changing the probabilistic interface
above.}

\p{For FTIP, a model artifact is not only the mathematical function
#{\pi_\theta}. It also carries a tokenizer, context convention, numerical
format, routing rule when experts are sparse, and an execution implementation.
These determine which prefixes are representable and how much training or
inference compute a policy evaluation costs.}
